course: biology
lesson: 4
title: The savanna: grass, trees and the great herds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A savanna is a warm grassland with trees scattered here and there, like the plains of the Serengeti in Africa, with a long dry season and a rainy season when the grass bursts back to life.
explain: Grass is the savanna's bread: zebras, wildebeest and gazelles graze it, and lions, cheetahs and hyenas hunt the grazers, so when the rain moves, the whole crowd moves after it.
explain: Every animal has its own floor in the savanna kitchen: the giraffe browses at treetop height, the elephant lower down, the zebra on the grass and the meerkat digs for beetles underneath.
explain: The Lion King opens with every animal bowing on Pride Rock; real herds keep well away from lions, Rafiki's mandrill-like face belongs to a rainforest monkey, and spotted hyenas are not silly scavengers but clever hunters led by females.
explain: On a hot afternoon at a safari park the lions lie flat in the shade like big sleepy cats: they hunt mostly at dusk, at night and at dawn, and resting in the heat saves their energy and water.
fact: Lions rest for up to about 20 hours a day, hunt mostly from dusk to dawn and live mainly on African grassland and savanna, not in jungles; a small group of Asiatic lions lives in India's Gir Forest.
fact: Giraffes are the tallest animals, up to about 5.5 metres, with a tongue about 45 cm long and just 7 neck bones, the same number as us, each over 25 cm long.
fact: Each year more than a million wildebeest, with zebras and gazelles, follow the rain and fresh grass in a loop round the Serengeti and the Masai Mara, even across crocodile rivers.
fact: Every zebra's stripes are different, the skin under the hair is dark, and strong evidence shows the stripes put off biting flies.
fact: The cheetah is the fastest land animal, reaching about 100 km per hour in short bursts, but it tires after a few hundred metres and often loses its catch to lions or hyenas.
fact: African elephants are the largest land animals, weighing about 6 tonnes or more; they spray dust and mud to protect their skin and flap their big ears to cool their blood.
fact: Termite mounds are built from soil, saliva and droppings, with tunnels that move air through them, and some are over a hundred years old.
fact: Spotted hyenas live in clans led by females, catch most of their own food and use whoops and giggles to talk; lions often steal from hyenas as well as the other way round.
fact: Whistling-thorn acacias give ants homes in swollen thorns and sweet nectar, and the ants attack animals that nibble the tree, so both win; when a giraffe keeps browsing, acacia leaves also soon turn bitter.
fact: A meerkat sentry stands guard while the group digs for beetles, scorpions and grubs, and it gives different alarm calls for an eagle in the air and a jackal on the ground.
fact: The ostrich is the largest living bird; it cannot fly but runs at about 70 km per hour on long legs with only two toes on each foot.
fact: Ostriches do not bury their heads in the sand; they lay their heads flat on the ground to hide and turn their eggs in a nest scraped in the soil.
fact: Baobab trees store huge amounts of water in their spongy trunks for the long dry season, and some have lived more than 1,500 years.
fact: Mandrills, the monkeys with red and blue faces, live in the rainforests of central Africa, not on the open savanna.
exercise: What is a savanna? | A warm grassland with scattered trees and a long dry season | A thick, dark forest where it rains hard every single day of the year | A cold, treeless plain near the North Pole | A sandy desert with no plants at all
exercise: When do lions mostly hunt? | At dusk, at night and around dawn | At midday, when the sun is highest and the prey is sleepy | Only in the rainy season | Never, they only eat what others leave behind
exercise: Where do most wild lions live? | On the grasslands and savannas of Africa | In the thick jungles of South America, near the rivers | In the rainforests of Borneo | In the deserts of Australia
exercise: How many bones are in a giraffe's neck? | 7, the same as in a human neck | About 50, because the neck is so very long | 1 very long bone | 14, twice as many as in a human
exercise: Why do the wildebeest herds travel so far each year? | They follow the rain and the fresh grass it grows | They are running away from the crocodiles in the rivers for good | They want to see new places | They go all the way to the sea to find salt
exercise: What does strong evidence say zebra stripes do? | They put off biting flies | They keep zebras warm at night | They help zebras find water | They make zebras run faster
exercise: What colour is a zebra's skin under its hair? | Dark | Pure white | Pink | Striped like the hair
exercise: Why do cheetahs often lose their catch? | They are tired after the sprint and too weak to fight lions or hyenas | They forget where they left it | They do not like the taste of meat | Vultures always get there first and eat the whole thing before the cheetah can return
exercise: Why does an elephant spray dust over its back? | To protect its skin from sun and insects and to stay cool | To clean itself, because it never bathes | To signal to other elephants far away across the plain | To hide its smell from lions
exercise: How do elephants use their big ears to stay cool? | They flap them to cool the blood flowing through them | They pour water from the river into them with their trunks | They fold them over their eyes for shade | They use them to fan the calves only
exercise: What is a termite mound made from? | Soil, termite saliva and droppings | Sticks and grass carried there by birds | Pure rock pushed up from under the ground | Elephant dung pushed into a big pile
exercise: Which is true about spotted hyenas? | They catch most of their own food and live in clans led by females | They only eat food that lions leave behind, and never hunt for themselves | They live alone and never make a sound | They are a kind of wild dog
exercise: What do ants do for a whistling-thorn acacia? | They attack animals that nibble its leaves | They carry its seeds all the way to the river | They eat its sharp thorns to keep it neat and tidy | They water it in the dry season with dew drops
exercise: What is the meerkat sentry doing on its mound? | Watching for eagles and jackals and calling an alarm | Sunbathing while all the others do the hard work | Looking for scorpions far away | Calling the whole group home for bedtime at sunset
exercise: How fast can an ostrich run? | About 70 km per hour | Only about 10 km per hour | About 200 km per hour | It cannot run at all, only walk
exercise: What does an ostrich really do with its head to hide? | Lays it flat on the ground | Buries it deep in the sand to hide | Tucks it under a wing to hide | Sticks it into a termite mound
exercise: Why does a baobab have such a fat trunk? | It stores water for the long dry season | It is hollow and full of nothing but air inside | It is full of fruit kept for hungry elephants | It holds the tree up when big floods come
test: «in the lion king rafiki's a mandrill, so mandrills live on the savanna» What is right? | No, real mandrills live in the rainforests of central Africa | Yes, they live on Pride Rock, just like in the film | Yes, they live in every grassland on Earth, from Africa to America | No, they live only in India
test: «hyenas are just thieves, they nick the lions' food» Which is the fairest answer? | Spotted hyenas catch most of their own food, and lions steal from them too | Yes, hyenas never catch anything themselves | Yes, and lions never steal from hyenas, because lions are the kings of the savanna | Hyenas only eat plants
test: «lion's king of the jungle innit, lives in the jungle» What is right? | Lions live mainly on savanna and grassland, not in jungles | Yes, lions live in the thick rainforest | Yes, and they sleep up in jungle trees | Lions live only in deserts
test: «ostrich got scared and stuck its head in the sand lol» What is right? | A myth: ostriches lay their heads flat on the ground to hide | True, they do it whenever they are scared | True, but only the males, to protect their eggs | A myth: they actually hide their heads up in the trees
test: What would happen to a cheetah that chased a gazelle for two kilometres at full speed? | It could not: it tires after a few hundred metres | It would catch the gazelle easily at the end | It would keep going for an hour without stopping to rest | It would fly the last part
test: Count them: two elephants and a giraffe at the waterhole. How many legs and how many trunks? | 12 legs and 2 trunks | 12 legs and 3 trunks | 10 legs and 2 trunks | 14 legs and 2 trunks
test: Count the toes: three ostriches stand together. How many toes on all their feet? | 12 | 18 | 30 | 6
test: «why'd the giraffe only eat 2 mouthfuls off that acacia and walk off» Which is the best answer? | The acacia makes its leaves bitter, and ants may attack too | The giraffe was not hungry at all that day | Giraffes only eat two leaves a day | The thorns are too long for its tongue to get round
test: «zebra's white with black stripes, everyone knows that» Which is closest to the truth? | Under the hair its skin is dark, and every zebra's pattern is different | All zebras have exactly the same stripes | Under the hair a zebra is white, and the black stripes are just painted on | Zebras can change their stripes each year
test: «shut up and tell me why the giraffe's neck's so long» Which is the best answer? | It reaches leaves high up, where other animals cannot compete | It helps the giraffe swim across rivers | It is for fighting crocodiles | It is long so the giraffe can sleep standing up without falling over
test: «why do the meerkat guards scream different noises» Which is the best answer? | Different calls warn of danger from the air or from the ground | They are singing to pass the time on guard | Each guard has only one call | They are calling the lions to come and eat the scorpions
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the savanna? | The savanna is a warm grassland bearing scattered trees and subject to a prolonged dry season | The savanna is a dense forest receiving precipitation upon every day of the year without exception | The savanna is a cold, treeless plain adjacent to the North Pole | The savanna is a sandy expanse wholly devoid of vegetation
exam: Identify the principal reason for the annual migration of wildebeest across the Serengeti and the Masai Mara. | The herds pursue the rainfall and the fresh grass which it engenders | The herds flee permanently from the crocodiles of the rivers | The herds seek salt from the sea, which they lack upon the plains of the interior | The herds are driven by herdsmen to market
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the cervical vertebrae of the giraffe. | The giraffe possesses seven cervical vertebrae, being the same number as a human | The giraffe possesses approximately fifty cervical vertebrae, owing to the length of its neck | The giraffe possesses a single elongated cervical vertebra | The giraffe possesses fourteen cervical vertebrae
exam: Ascertain the conduct of the ostrich when it seeks concealment. | The ostrich lays its head and neck flat upon the ground | The ostrich buries its head beneath the sand | The ostrich conceals its head beneath a wing | The ostrich ascends a tree
